 work it is still less  efficient with engineering time than  there's waiting until you can do the  real thing and of course you you can  write a shim to unblock yourself the  trick here is to be absolutely certain  that you can ship this shim if necessary  as a fallback but even then it's still  probably a bad idea I really don't  recommend you do it the next lesson  we're going to talk about is how to  handle content scaling the volume of  source content in your game is hard to  predict ahead of time but it's very  important to try and predict it because  it has a big impact on the scalability  of your content processing pipeline but  one thing we can say for sure it tends  to arrive very quickly one reason for  this is your contents team is likely to  ramp up exponentially as you move into  production particularly if you're part  of a multiple projects you do you can go  from a small number of content creators  to a large number of very very fast  another factor is the complexity of  individual assets it's very possible to  build a combinatorial system that has  complexity proportional to for example  models times shaders times permutations  and not really realize that that's what  you've done and your content will go  through several changes and fidelity as  you go from mass out to production and  then from production to  production so for example you add more  shaders put a model may permutations  model you can end up with really large  increases in Asset complexity they  happen across your whole content tree  very very quickly the last reason is the  artists tend to travel in packs at least  at Bungie our audits are very very good  at bringing a whole team to bear on a  single experience and making a look  amazing  like an e3 demo something like that this  also can increase your asset complexity  very quickly so all these factors  multiplied together to do something like  this everyone raise your hands if this  gap graph gave you a flashback to some  disaster in your career Wow less than I  thought  okay just like climate change this is a  problem our monkey brains are very bad  at solving by the time you are in the  danger zone it is already too late to  fight these trends you are stuck trying  to mitigate and you're unable to keep  pace with the incoming volume of content  that means every engine must think about  this problem ahead of time and try to  get ahead of the curve the big problem  here is that content scales super  linearly but ancient capability tends to  scale sub linearly many operations used  in graph systems scale is order n log n  which is not great you can try to use  distributed computing but you quickly  run into Amdahl's law so what you have  to do is aggressively design your  pipeline to scale linearly that means  decouple your content types and try to  break dependencies between them  sometimes you may have to choose kind of  a dumb content pipeline and push a bit  more work to the runtime engine or to  the content creator the goal is to keep  several types of content isolated from  one another and prevent crosstalk that  may not be convenient for your content  creator you may be able to solve that in  your source editor environment but it's  usually the right thing to reduce the  overall system complexity another  strategy is that sometimes you can get  your content creators to accept the  partial workflow for example rather than  implementing an expensive incremental  lighting update system maybe just give  your artist of the choice of whether  they want to bake light maps where they  want to use the old ones all of these  strategies basically boil down to trying  to avoid global optimization problems  because they don't scale many of these  principles are the same principles that  govern server scalability you must look  at your content processing pipeline and  think about how can we scale this out  rather than into scale up these single  points of global optimization  if you can do that you can stay closer  to closer to a linear process and  capability increase and that means you  can spend more of your time in a sweet  spot underneath the content curve so now  let's finish up by talking about the  epic struggle against software  complexity so far I've touched on this  number of times in passing but really is  the entire theme of this presentation  software complexity manifests itself as  this feeling that it's kind of hard to  get things done I'm not talking about  technical debt here which is bad code at  the leaf of your engine but rather  structural complexity one of the source  of this complexity will was just adding  engineers I have this kind of principle  that number of lines of code is equal to  number of engineers times number of  months and it has almost no relation to  the problem you actually ask them to  solve in the first place  modern engines also have many  specialized systems for example the  Destiny shader system about a hundred  times more lines of code than the halo  shade of system that shipped back in  2001 another big source of complexity is  that in the modern engine some systems  are never really finished and that  incurs attacks on everything around them  due to edge cases error handling or just  clumsy api's another source is a lack of  inspection tools what that means is if  something goes wrong you have to dive  deep into all this complexity which  slows down your ability to understand  what's going on all of this complexity  can lead to a positive feedback loop  right it's hard to get things done you  just have to get your code written as  quickly as possible you don't have time  for the right way that leads to more  complexity your iteration speed is slow  so you make a smaller number of large  chickens that means you have fewer  chances to increment alehrer fine your  code which leads to more complexity and  so the cycle just continues around and  around unless you are continuously  paying attention of this you can get  stuck in a very inefficient State how do  we deal with this we deal with this by  making sure we have a focus on  architecture we have a really defined  software architecture yet but I believe  that we can software architecture is the  large-scale structure of a code base  okay  well but what's strong architecture this  is structure that controls complexity  within your engine it's a set of  interfaces that allow you to insulate  systems from each other's implementation  details it's the idea of a watertight  abstraction rather than a leaky  abstraction  heids internal complexity and presents a  simple outward reliable wrapper to other  engine other other components it's a set  of utility technologies that let you  quickly understand what's going on  within your engine you know I could go  on and on this is well-documented we all  kind of know what strong architecture  looks like but what's the benefit of  strong architecture what doesn't really  let you build features faster well it  does a little bit because iteration  speed it doesn't really increase the  quality of your game except a little bit  again because of iteration speed but  what it lets you do is tackle big  problems problems that would otherwise  be out of reach entirely if you tackle  big problems without having strong  architecture you wind up drowning and  complexity and unable to complete your  feature no matter how long you spend  doing it there's just one problem with  this like we already established  changing the rules of a codebase is hard  and code bases are really large these  days we're working with gigantic code  bases under shifting requirements and  constant pressures of expediency and we  don't get to press the reset button  these investments in our code are just  too large to throw away so what I  believe is this means it is literally  impossible for us to design and then  build large-scale strong architecture  from the ground up not anymore those  days are now behind us now we are no  longer software architects what are we  we are software gardeners  we work in environments that are messy  they are full of sprawling rich life and  complexity we spend our days grappling  with the limits of our ability to affect  change we don't really get to build new  things anymore but there is still a joy  to be found in the work and so I say  make pragmatic decisions work with what  you have stay in the moment but keep one  eye on the future and don't be afraid to  make big investments be brave when we  look back on destiny we built a lot of  great technology and shipped a pretty  great game we knew from the start that  it would be tough and it was but it was  absolutely worth it I firmly believe you  cannot build something great without by  staying safe anyway I hope that our  experiences can help all of you and  taking on your own big challenges and  then completing them successfully thanks  a lot for listening
 

